WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.440
MAX SALTONSTALL: On this
episode of "Stack Chat,"

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.420
we welcome Matt Brown,
infrastructure engineer

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:04.420
at Spotify.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.360
Hear how Kubernetes
played a key role

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:07.860
in making the
migration of Spotify's

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.720
back-end microservices
as seamless

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.310
as possible for the
200-plus teams involved.

00:00:15.193 --> 00:00:16.360
Thanks for joining me, Matt.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:19.660
So why did you choose
Kubernetes over what

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:21.732
I'm sure were many other
alternatives for you?

00:00:21.732 --> 00:00:24.190
MATT BROWN: So we've been using
containers for a long time,

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:28.040
since about 2013, since
Docker was very new.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:31.720
We had built our own
container orchestration system

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.630
around 2013, before there really
was other choices out there.

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:37.450
And we named it Helios.

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:39.490
And we open-sourced
it, coincidentally,

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:41.500
the day before
Kubernetes was announced.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.000
So we didn't really
know at the time

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.560
that there was something
else coming that we'd use.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.380
And so we knew, I
think, for a long time,

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.880
we didn't want to continue to
be in the business of building

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:51.730
our own orchestration platform.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:53.690
Kubernetes is a wonderful
open-source project--

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:56.740
a huge amount of
contributors, great velocity.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:58.990
And so we knew eventually
we wanted to move over to it

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:03.410
just to get a lot more features,
and impact from the community.

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:07.480
And we think, in the end, the
orchestration platform that

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.610
will have, this
thing, the offering

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:11.277
we can make to our
own engineering team,

00:01:11.277 --> 00:01:12.735
is going to be so
much better if we

00:01:12.735 --> 00:01:15.010
can be a part of this big
open-source movement instead

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:17.260
of having a team of
four or five engineers

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.030
building our own
thing internally.

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:20.445
We thought it would take--

00:01:20.445 --> 00:01:21.820
or at least I
thought initially--

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:23.403
we'd have to do a
lot of selling, kind

00:01:23.403 --> 00:01:26.050
of talking, nudging people
along, pushing people along,

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:27.053
at some point.

00:01:27.053 --> 00:01:28.720
But we found that
most teams were pretty

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.133
motivated to adopt Kubernetes.

00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:32.800
MAX SALTONSTALL: So
how did you approach

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.370
doing this transition from
your custom-made orchestration

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:38.200
to GKE over time?

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.110
MATT BROWN: A big principle we
had for trying to figure out,

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:43.950
how are we going to move
over, was trying to--

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:46.630
we really wanted to make the
migration for each engineering

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:48.330
team at Spotify as
easy as possible.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:53.900
But we feared if we had to
teach every team, times 200

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.530
or so teams, how do
I use Kubernetes,

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:58.810
or if it was a completely
different system from what

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:00.010
they use today--

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:02.680
different metric
systems, different ways

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.510
to do logging, et cetera--

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:06.880
or if the two systems
couldn't talk to each other,

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.190
then the migration project
would be never-ending.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:12.190
Because Spotify's
back-end is organized

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.498
into hundreds of
thousands of microservices

00:02:14.498 --> 00:02:16.290
from different teams
talking to each other.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:18.832
And so one thing we really were
afraid of and wanted to avoid

00:02:18.832 --> 00:02:22.300
was, in order for my team to
move my service to Kubernetes,

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.010
I need to organize with the
other teams calling my service,

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:26.935
and we all have to move
it at the same time.

00:02:26.935 --> 00:02:28.810
That would have led to
a ton of dependencies,

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:31.720
and would have taken many, many
years to move one service over,

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:33.040
let alone 1,000.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.130
We had built a system on top
of Helios that we call Tugboat.

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:40.635
And it really just gives a
simpler interface and API.

00:02:40.635 --> 00:02:42.010
And we realized,
well, we already

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:45.345
have almost every service at
Spotify talking to this API.

00:02:45.345 --> 00:02:47.720
And basically, they can just
say, they can tell Tugboat--

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.178
I have a new version
of my service.

00:02:49.178 --> 00:02:51.227
Please go deploy it
out to the world.

00:02:51.227 --> 00:02:52.810
And it gives a much
simpler interface.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:54.310
Helios was a little
bit low-level.

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:55.810
And so we realized,
well, if we just

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:58.493
add Kubernetes as another
option to this system,

00:02:58.493 --> 00:03:00.410
we already have everything
integrated with it.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:03.850
If we wanted to find a new
next-generation deployment

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:06.148
platform, for example,
we'd have to do

00:03:06.148 --> 00:03:07.690
all the work of
integrating everybody

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:08.990
with it all over again.

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:11.770
So we just basically added
Kubernetes as another option

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:12.573
to Tugboat.

00:03:12.573 --> 00:03:14.740
MAX SALTONSTALL: How did
you weigh the pros and cons

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:20.500
of Kubernetes with your own
management versus managed GKE

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:21.560
deployment?

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:24.310
MATT BROWN: So over the years,
as we were trying to think,

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:26.920
when is a good time to start
seriously adopting Kubernetes--

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.420
and we did want to
wait for a while,

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:31.270
because Helios helped
us make great strides,

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:33.610
and it seemed like Kubernetes
was still kind of growing,

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:37.692
still figuring a lot of things
out, just like any project--

00:03:37.692 --> 00:03:39.400
as we were doing that
and kind of waiting

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.150
for when is the right time, we
were moving our whole back-end

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:45.100
to Google Cloud anyway, and
getting rid of our own data

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:45.950
centers.

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:49.030
So there wasn't really much of
an argument to be had to say,

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:50.860
like, we want to build
our own clusters,

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:52.443
learn how to do all
of the networking,

00:03:52.443 --> 00:03:55.193
do all the stuff
ourselves the hard way.

00:03:55.193 --> 00:03:56.860
One of our main
motivations for adopting

00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:59.410
Google Cloud in general
was just to take advantage

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:01.360
of many managed services,
and so our teams

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:03.880
can kind of focus
on upper layers

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:05.688
and spend less time
on lower-level things.

00:04:05.688 --> 00:04:07.730
MAX SALTONSTALL: So then
with all these services,

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:10.210
how do you make sure
that, as they're shifting,

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:12.340
they can still collect
metrics and understand

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:15.370
their context within that
Spotify microservices

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:16.042
ecosystem?

00:04:16.042 --> 00:04:18.459
MATT BROWN: We did a lot of
work as an infrastructure team

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:23.110
to make sure that if your
team is building a container,

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:25.450
the things that you would
see in the environment

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:28.000
in our old system
in Helios would look

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.990
mostly the same in Kubernetes.

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:32.268
And that's things
like, service discovery

00:04:32.268 --> 00:04:33.310
would be handled for you.

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:35.980
That was one of the
features of Helios.

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:38.110
Metrics were provided
by just a process

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:41.480
running on a known port on the
same machine as your container.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:43.160
So you could just
send metrics to that.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.400
And we had similar things
for secrets and logging.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:46.900
So we did a lot of
work to make sure

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:50.110
that all of that looked the
same in Kubernetes, so that you,

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:52.542
as an application, or what
we call a feature team,

00:04:52.542 --> 00:04:54.500
really didn't have to
rewrite your code at all.

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:55.820
You can just take
your container,

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:58.090
which is already packaged
up, and move it to the new--

00:04:58.090 --> 00:04:59.185
move it to Kubernetes.

00:04:59.185 --> 00:05:01.060
MAX SALTONSTALL: If you
enjoyed this episode,

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:03.400
check out the "Stack Chat"
playlist for more videos.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:07.350
